# Example OpenSSH known_hosts file for NCM's ssh.known_hosts_path.
#
# Every key blob below is RANDOM, FAKE DATA — it is NOT a real host key and
# will not authenticate against any real device. The IPs/hostnames use
# RFC 5737 documentation ranges (198.51.100.0/24) and RFC 2606 reserved
# domains (.example.net) so nothing here resembles real production infra.
# This file exists to show the real FORMAT, filled in, not just a bracketed
# template — copy it, then replace every IP/hostname and regenerate the key
# material for real with ssh-keyscan (see README.md in this directory).
# Always verify each fingerprint out-of-band before trusting it.
#
# Lines starting with '#' and blank lines are ignored by OpenSSH's known_hosts
# parser, same as this file demonstrates.

# --- Plain entry, keyed by IP address ---
# Format: <host-or-ip>[,<alias>] <key-type> <base64-public-key> [comment]
# This matches the device used in conf.yaml in this same directory.
198.51.100.10 ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIhSTSc3DR8k2vHBazi5nJrg56/nhbEKLVd2EbcSBpL7F core-switch-01

# --- Plain entry, keyed by hostname instead of IP ---
# Useful if you connect by hostname rather than ip_address in conf.yaml,
# though NCM's conf.yaml uses ip_address, so matching by IP (above) is the
# common case. Older platforms may only offer RSA host keys.
edge-router-01.example.net ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2EAAAADAQABAAABgQDWYmx0GOow8FYqXW5oAmUdq8bzWII532ZJRVya2ly+E9vaxLbbbR6o8RNLt2KHhjljMbatEuaak21wxM2pyiLmD1Q4FzjT3VBAsPSrxfPi4dTIWzQwV7wUOhcqFT9nKQx7an

# --- Multiple hostnames/aliases for the same key, comma-separated ---
198.51.100.11,leaf-switch-01,leaf-switch-01.example.net ecdsa-sha2-nistp256 AAAAE2VjZHNhLXNoYTItbmlzdHAyNTYAAAAIbmlzdHAyNTYAAABBBPmpofHv6HWUgkp3J97yxNH0e4TSdi7ou7pCTGHmIhvSEZYFbdhUFYcxCfMVn+8xeviFBFR/3EYm1NG0PWhfaYYT4oNQ/vK9D

# --- Hashed hostname entry (ssh-keyscan -H output) ---
# The leading "|1|<salt>|<hash>" form hides the actual hostname/IP in the
# file, so a leaked known_hosts doesn't reveal your device inventory. This
# is what `ssh-keyscan -H` produces (see README.md) and is the recommended
# form for anything beyond a small lab. This example hashes 198.51.100.12.
|1|nfvH6S1ucvXleKdS4UU/5h7ijSA=|PbiNiQQ2bQg4rInKbbfhdTalpzE= ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIhSTSc3DR8k2vHBazi5nJrg56/nhbEKLVd2EbcSBpL7F

# --- Non-default SSH port, bracket notation ---
# If a device listens on a non-standard SSH port, the host portion is
# bracketed with the port appended, matching OpenSSH's own convention.
# This example is 198.51.100.13 on port 2222.
[198.51.100.13]:2222 ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIhSTSc3DR8k2vHBazi5nJrg56/nhbEKLVd2EbcSBpL7F
